1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,787
Chapter 3: Congenital Genitourinary Abnormalities.

3
00:00:06,787 --> 00:00:17,162
Genitourinary Tract Development Sexual Differentiation Disorders Of Sex Development Bladder And Perineal Abnormalities MÜLLERIAN ABNORMALITIES Uterine Flexion.

4
00:00:17,162 --> 00:00:18,925
References.

5
00:00:18,925 --> 00:00:29,712
In females, the external genitalia, gonads, and müllerian ducts each derive from different primordia and in close association with the urinary tract and hindgut.

6
00:00:29,712 --> 00:00:38,975
Abnormal embryogenesis can lead to reproductive organs that predispose to infertility, subfertility, miscarriage, or preterm delivery.

7
00:00:38,975 --> 00:00:41,975
Genitourinary Tract Development.

8
00:00:41,975 --> 00:00:44,550
# Urinary System.

9
00:00:44,550 --> 00:00:54,050
Between the 3rd and 5th gestational weeks, an elevation of intermediate mesoderm on each side of the fetus begins to develop into the urogenital tract.

10
00:00:54,050 --> 00:01:01,825
This urogenital ridge divides into the genital ridge, destined to become the gonads, and into the nephrogenic ridge .

11
00:01:01,825 --> 00:01:06,912
Each nephrogenic ridge produces a mesonephros (mesonephric kidney).

12
00:01:06,912 --> 00:01:19,225
Recall that evolution of the renal system passes sequentially from the mesonephric stage to reach the permanent metanephric system (de Bakker, 2019; Upadhyay, 2014).

13
00:01:19,225 --> 00:01:29,100
Each nephrogenic ridge also gives rise to a mesonephric duct, also termed wolffian duct, and to a paramesonephric duct, also called müllerian duct.

14
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:31,312
Figure 3-1.

15
00:01:31,312 --> 00:01:35,325
A. Cross-section of an embryo at 4 to 6 weeks.

16
00:01:35,325 --> 00:01:43,775
B. Primordial germ cells migrate (arrows) from the yolk sac to the area of germinal epithelium, within the genital ridge.

17
00:01:43,775 --> 00:01:46,300
C. Development of gonad.

18
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:52,975
Also, migration of sympathetic cells from the spinal ganglia to a region above the developing kidney.

19
00:01:52,975 --> 00:01:58,900
The early urinary tract develops from the mesonephric ducts (Fig. 3-2A).

20
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:07,725
Between the 4th and 5th weeks, each mesonephric duct gives rise to a ureteric bud, which grows cephalad (Fig. 3-2B).

21
00:02:07,725 --> 00:02:16,225
As each bud lengthens, it induces differentiation of the metanephros, which will become the final kidney (Fig. 3-2C) .

22
00:02:16,225 --> 00:02:23,812
The metanephros ascends to its final position by the 9th week because of disproportionate growth of the embryo’s caudal region .

23
00:02:23,812 --> 00:02:30,862
Each ureteric bud also gives rise to an elongation that becomes the metanephric duct or future ureter.

24
00:02:30,862 --> 00:02:33,075
Figure 3-2.

25
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:38,112
Embryonic development of the female genitourinary tract (A–F).

26
00:02:38,112 --> 00:02:52,237
Reproduced with permission from Shatzkes DR, Haller JO, Velcek FT: Imaging of uterovaginal anomalies in the pediatric patient, Urol Radiol 1991;13(1):58–66.

27
00:02:52,237 --> 00:02:59,112
The cloaca begins as a common opening for the embryonic urinary, genital, and alimentary tracts .

28
00:02:59,112 --> 00:03:07,887
By the 7th week, it is divided by the urorectal septum to create the hindgut and the urogenital sinus (Fig. 3-2D) .

29
00:03:07,887 --> 00:03:29,362
The urogenital sinus is considered in three parts: (1) the cephalad or vesicle portion, which forms the urinary bladder; (2) the middle or pelvic portion, which creates the female urethra; and (3) the caudal or phallic part, which gives rise to the distal vagina and to the greater vestibular (Bartholin) and paraurethral glands.

30
00:03:29,362 --> 00:03:37,737
Near the end of the first trimester, each mesonephros degenerates, and without testosterone, the mesonephric ducts regress as well.

31
00:03:37,737 --> 00:03:44,650
The ureterovesical junction forms from incorporation of the metanephric ducts into the bladder at the trigone.

32
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:50,262
Abnormalities of this process lead to obstruction and vesicoureteral reflux .

33
00:03:50,262 --> 00:03:52,725
# Genital Tract.

34
00:03:52,725 --> 00:04:00,187
The fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper vagina derive from the müllerian ducts (see Fig. 3-2B).

35
00:04:00,187 --> 00:04:11,400
Linear spatial development of these organs along the duct length is guided by several genes and notably by Hox genes (Du, 2004; Jacquinet, 2016).

36
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:17,137
These ducts extend downward and then turn medially to meet and fuse together in the midline.

37
00:04:17,137 --> 00:04:24,637
The uterus is formed by this union of the two müllerian ducts at approximately the 10th week (Fig. 3-2E) .

38
00:04:24,637 --> 00:04:30,525
Fusion to create the uterus begins in the middle and then extends both caudally and cephalad.

39
00:04:30,525 --> 00:04:38,337
With cellular proliferation at the upper portion, a thick wedge of tissue creates the characteristic piriform uterine shape.

40
00:04:38,337 --> 00:04:45,962
At the same time, dissolution of cells at the lower pole forms the first uterine cavity (Fig. 3-2F).

41
00:04:45,962 --> 00:04:53,675
As the upper wedge-shaped septum is slowly reabsorbed, the final uterine cavity is usually formed by the 20th week

42
00:04:53,836 --> 00:04:59,173
If the two müllerian ducts fail to fuse, two separate uterine horns remain.

43
00:04:59,123 --> 00:05:06,961
In contrast, resorption failure of the common tissue between them results in various degrees of persistent uterine septum.

44
00:05:06,961 --> 00:05:17,673
As the distal end of the fused müllerian ducts contacts the urogenital sinus, this induces endodermal outgrowths from the sinus, which are termed the sinovaginal bulbs.

45
00:05:17,673 --> 00:05:24,711
These bulbs proliferate and fuse to form the vaginal plate, which later resorbs to form the vaginal lumen.

46
00:05:24,711 --> 00:05:29,248
Vaginal canalization is generally completed by the 20th week .

47
00:05:29,248 --> 00:05:35,361
However, the lumen remains separated from the urogenital sinus by the hymeneal membrane.

48
00:05:35,361 --> 00:05:39,736
This membrane further degenerates to leave only the hymeneal ring.

49
00:05:39,736 --> 00:05:50,886
The close association of the mesonephric (wolffian) and paramesonephric (müllerian) ducts explains the potential to see simultaneous abnormalities in their end organs.

50
00:05:50,886 --> 00:06:04,873
Nearly half of females with uterovaginal malformations have associated urinary tract defects, and this association is explored later (p. 40) (Kenney, 1984; Semmens, 1962).

51
00:06:04,873 --> 00:06:12,786
With müllerian anomalies, ovaries are functionally normal but have a higher incidence of anatomical maldescent into the pelvis .

52
00:06:12,786 --> 00:06:17,798
As discussed, the mesonephric ducts usually degenerate in the female.

53
00:06:17,798 --> 00:06:22,211
However, persistent remnants may become clinically apparent.

54
00:06:22,211 --> 00:06:27,736
Mesonephric, that is wolffian, vestiges can persist as Gartner duct cysts.

55
00:06:27,736 --> 00:06:35,473
These are typically located in the proximal anterolateral vaginal wall but may be found at other sites along the vaginal length.

56
00:06:35,473 --> 00:06:42,161
Most cysts are asymptomatic and benign and usually do not require surgical excision or drainage.

57
00:06:42,161 --> 00:06:54,448
Intraabdominal wolffian remnants in the female include a few blind tubules in the mesovarium—the epoöphoron—and similar ones adjacent to the uterus—paroöphoron (see Fig. 3-2F) .

58
00:06:54,448 --> 00:07:00,836
The epoöphoron or paroöphoron may develop into clinically identifiable benign cysts in the adult.

59
00:07:00,836 --> 00:07:03,023
# Gonads.

60
00:07:03,023 --> 00:07:10,948
Because of separate gonadal and müllerian derivation, women with müllerian defects typically have functionally normal ovaries.

61
00:07:10,948 --> 00:07:17,698
At approximately 4 weeks, gonads form from the genital ridge, also known as the gonadal ridge.

62
00:07:17,698 --> 00:07:24,273
This ridge forms from the coelomic epithelium covering the medioventral surface of the mesonephros .

63
00:07:24,273 --> 00:07:30,848
Recall that coelomic epithelium arises from mesoderm and invests the body cavity’s inner surface .

64
00:07:30,848 --> 00:07:38,011
Next, strands of these epithelial cells extend into the underlying mesenchyme as the primary sex cords.

65
00:07:38,011 --> 00:07:43,498
Another gonadal component is the primordial germ cell, the future oogonia.

66
00:07:43,498 --> 00:07:58,373
By the 6th week, primordial germ cells have migrated from the yolk sac along the dorsal mesentery to enter the genital ridge mesenchyme (see Fig. 3-1) (Fujimoto, 1977; Hen, 2019).

67
00:07:58,373 --> 00:08:03,636
The primordial germ cells are then incorporated into the primary sex cords.

68
00:08:03,636 --> 00:08:07,361
In the 7th week, the sexes can be distinguished.

69
00:08:07,361 --> 00:08:16,198
Testes are recognized during microscopic sectioning by their well-defined radiating testis cords, which derived from the primary sex cords.

70
00:08:16,198 --> 00:08:20,961
These cords develop into the seminiferous tubules and rete testis.

71
00:08:20,961 --> 00:08:26,023
The rete testis connects with small tubes arising off the mesonephric duct.

72
00:08:26,023 --> 00:08:35,098
These small tubes become the efferent ducts that drain into the epididymis and then into the vas deferens, which are main mesonephric duct derivatives.

73
00:08:35,098 --> 00:08:39,523
After the 8th week, gonads begin to differ grossly as well .

74
00:08:39,523 --> 00:08:47,361
In the female embryo, the primary sex cords give rise to the medullary cords, which persist only for a short time.

75
00:08:47,361 --> 00:08:54,873
The coelomic epithelium again proliferates into the underlying mesenchyme, and these strands are the cortical cords.

76
00:08:54,873 --> 00:09:02,073
By the 16th week, the cortical cords begin to form isolated cell clusters called primordial follicles.

77
00:09:02,073 --> 00:09:12,648
These follicles contain the oogonia, which derive from primordial germ cells, and a single surrounding layer of follicular cells, derived from the cortical cords.

78
00:09:12,648 --> 00:09:16,248
Follicular cells are supporting nutrient cells.

79
00:09:16,248 --> 00:09:24,336
By 8 months, the ovary has become a long, narrow, lobulated structure that is attached to the body wall by the mesovarium.

80
00:09:24,336 --> 00:09:32,123
The coelomic epithelium has been separated by a band of connective tissue—the tunica albuginea— from the cortex.

81
00:09:32,123 --> 00:09:33,523
At

82
00:09:33,676 --> 00:09:44,138
this stage, the cortex contains follicles and is well defined from the inner medulla, which is composed of abundant blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and nerve fibers.

83
00:09:44,088 --> 00:09:46,963
# External Genitalia.

84
00:09:46,963 --> 00:09:51,963
Early development of the external genitalia is similar in both sexes.

85
00:09:51,963 --> 00:09:58,701
By 6 weeks’ gestation, three external protuberances have developed surrounding the cloacal membrane.

86
00:09:58,701 --> 00:10:06,301
These are the left and right cloacal folds, which meet ventrally to form the genital tubercle (Fig. 3-3A).

87
00:10:06,301 --> 00:10:16,776
With division of the cloacal membrane into anal and urogenital membranes in the early 7th week, the cloacal folds become the anal and urethral folds, respectively.

88
00:10:16,776 --> 00:10:23,501
Lateral to the urethral folds, genital swellings arise, and these become the labioscrotal folds.

89
00:10:23,501 --> 00:10:32,176
Between the urethral folds, the urogenital sinus extends onto the surface of the enlarging genital tubercle to form the urethral groove.

90
00:10:32,176 --> 00:10:40,413
Late in week 7, the urogenital membrane ruptures, exposing the cavity of the urogenital sinus to amnionic fluid.

91
00:10:40,413 --> 00:10:42,663
Figure 3-3.

92
00:10:42,663 --> 00:10:45,763
Development of the external genitalia.

93
00:10:45,763 --> 00:10:48,151
A. Indifferent stage.

94
00:10:48,151 --> 00:10:51,601
B. Virilization of external genitalia.

95
00:10:51,601 --> 00:10:53,851
C. Feminization.

96
00:10:53,851 --> 00:10:59,713
The genital tubercle elongates to form the phallus in males and the clitoris in females.

97
00:10:59,713 --> 00:11:07,263
Still, it is not possible to visually differentiate between male and female external genitalia until week 12.

98
00:11:07,263 --> 00:11:15,076
In the male fetus, dihydrotestosterone (DHT) forms locally by the 5-α reduction of testosterone.

99
00:11:15,076 --> 00:11:25,501
DHT prompts the anogenital distance to lengthen, the phallus to enlarge, and the labioscrotal folds to fuse and form the scrotum (see Fig. 33B).

100
00:11:25,501 --> 00:11:36,351
In the female fetus, without DHT, the anogenital distance does not lengthen, and the labioscrotal and urethral folds do not fuse (see Fig. 3-3C).

101
00:11:36,351 --> 00:11:43,988
The genital tubercle bends caudally to become the clitoris, and the urogenital sinus forms the vestibule of the vagina.

102
00:11:43,988 --> 00:11:51,176
The labioscrotal folds create the labia majora, whereas the urethral folds persist as the labia minora.

103
00:11:51,176 --> 00:12:00,388
Female external genitals are differentiated by 11 weeks, whereas male external genital differentiation is complete by 14 weeks.

104
00:12:00,388 --> 00:12:12,151
To differentiate phenotypic gender early, first-trimester sonography relies on the angle of the genital tubercle off a horizontal line drawn parallel to the lumbosacral skin surface .

105
00:12:12,151 --> 00:12:20,588
Specifically, male gender is assigned if the angle is >30°, and female gender if the angle is <10°.

106
00:12:20,588 --> 00:12:22,888
Figure 3-4.

107
00:12:22,888 --> 00:12:32,813
First-trimester transvaginal sonogram shows a male fetus, whose phallus lies at an angle that is ≥30 degrees above the horizontal.

108
00:12:32,813 --> 00:12:37,088
The horizontal line aligns with the fetal lumbosacral skin.

109
00:12:37,088 --> 00:12:40,801
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Ashley Zink.

110
00:12:40,801 --> 00:12:43,276
Sexual Differentiation.

111
00:12:43,276 --> 00:12:49,438
Defining gender incorporates genetic gender, gonadal gender, and phenotypic gender.

112
00:12:49,438 --> 00:12:54,776
Genetic gender—XX or XY—is established at fertilization.

113
00:12:54,776 --> 00:13:01,601
However, for the first 6 weeks, male and female embryos are morphologically indistinguishable.

114
00:13:01,601 --> 00:13:08,351
Gonadal gender is heralded by the differentiation of the primordial gonad into a testis or an ovary.

115
00:13:08,351 --> 00:13:13,801
If a Y chromosome is present, the gonad begins developing into a testis.

116
00:13:13,801 --> 00:13:22,026
Testis development is directed by the sexdetermining region (SRY) gene, located on the short arm of the Y chromosome .

117
00:13:22,026 --> 00:13:34,251
In addition, testis development requires other autosomal genes that include SOX9, WT1, DAX1, WNT4, and NR5A1(SF1) .

118
00:13:34,251 --> 00:13:40,876
Identified mutations in these and others are linked to disorders of sex development, described next.

119
00:13:40,876 --> 00:13:46,701
The importance of the SRY gene is demonstrated in several paradoxical conditions.

120
00:13:46,701 --> 00:13:58,751
For example, 46,XX phenotypic males can result from translocation of the Y chromosome fragment containing SRY to the X chromosome during meiosis of male germ cells .

121
00:13:58,751 --> 00:14:07,026
Similarly, 46,XY individ-uals can appear phenotypically female if they carry a mutation in the SRY gene .

122
00:14:07,026 --> 00:14:11,538
Last, phenotypic gender begins at 8 weeks’ gestation.

123
00:14:11,538 --> 00:14:16,938
Before this, urogenital tract development in both sexes is homologous.

124
00:14:16,938 --> 00:14:26,001
Thereafter, differentiation of the internal and external genitalia to the male phenotype is dependent on testicular function and response.

125
00:14:26,001 --> 00:14:31,676
In its absence, female differentiation ensues irrespective of genetic gender .

126
00:14:31,676 --> 00:14:33,888
Table 3-1.

127
00:14:33,888 --> 00:14:35,651
Embryonic

128
00:14:35,812 --> 00:14:49,312
Urogenital Structures and Their Adult Homologues In males, the Sertoli cells of the fetal testis secrete a protein called antimüllerian hormone (AMH), also named müllerian-inhibiting substance (MIS).

129
00:14:49,262 --> 00:14:58,187
It acts locally as a paracrine factor to cause müllerian duct regression (Grinspon, 2020; Mäkelä, 2019).

130
00:14:58,187 --> 00:15:03,949
Thus, it prevents development of the uterus, fallopian tube, and upper vagina.

131
00:15:03,949 --> 00:15:11,237
Sertoli cells secrete AMH before differentiation of Leydig cells, which synthesize testosterone.

132
00:15:11,237 --> 00:15:18,612
AMH is secreted as early as 7 weeks, and müllerian duct regression is completed by 9 to 10 weeks.

133
00:15:18,612 --> 00:15:31,299
Because AMH acts locally near its site of formation, if a testis were absent on one side, the müllerian duct on that side would persist, and the uterine horn and fallopian tube would develop on that side.

134
00:15:31,299 --> 00:15:43,137
Through stimulation initially by human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), and later by fetal pituitary luteinizing hormone (LH), Leydig cells secrete testosterone.

135
00:15:43,137 --> 00:15:51,312
This hormone acts directly on the wolffian duct to promote development of the vas deferens, epididymis, and seminal vesicles.

136
00:15:51,312 --> 00:15:56,624
Testosterone also enters fetal blood and acts on the external genitalia.

137
00:15:56,624 --> 00:16:04,449
In these tissues, testosterone is converted to 5α-DHT to cause virilization of the external genitalia.

138
00:16:04,449 --> 00:16:12,562
Leydig cells also produce insulin-like factor 3, which prompts embryonic testes to descend by acting on the gubernaculum .

139
00:16:12,562 --> 00:16:15,137
Disorders Of Sex Development.

140
00:16:15,137 --> 00:16:17,487
# Definitions.

141
00:16:17,487 --> 00:16:24,137
Abnormal sex development may involve the gonads, internal duct system, or external genitalia.

142
00:16:24,137 --> 00:16:37,974
Current classification of disorders of sex development (DSDs) include: (1) sex chromosome DSDs, (2) 46,XY DSDs, and (3) 46,XX DSDs .

143
00:16:37,974 --> 00:16:44,524
Rates vary depending on included entities and approximate 1 case in every 5000 births .

144
00:16:44,524 --> 00:16:46,749
Table 3-2.

145
00:16:46,749 --> 00:16:54,312
Disorders of Sex Development (DSD) Classification Other terms describe abnormal phenotypic findings.

146
00:16:54,312 --> 00:17:00,874
First, some DSDs have abnormal, underdeveloped gonads, that is, gonadal dysgenesis.

147
00:17:00,874 --> 00:17:05,662
With this, a poorly formed testis is called a dysgenetic testis.

148
00:17:05,662 --> 00:17:09,062
A poorly formed ovary is a streak gonad.

149
00:17:09,062 --> 00:17:19,537
Underdeveloped gonads ultimately fail, which creates low sex steroid hormone levels but elevated follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and LH levels.

150
00:17:19,537 --> 00:17:26,687
A second term, ambiguous genitalia, describes genitalia that do not appear clearly male or female.

151
00:17:26,687 --> 00:17:36,137
Abnormalities can include hypospadias, undescended testes, micropenis or enlarged clitoris, labial fusion, and labial mass.

152
00:17:36,137 --> 00:17:43,862
Last, ovotesticular defines a rare state characterized by ovarian and testicular tissue in the same individual.

153
00:17:43,862 --> 00:17:47,424
It was formerly termed true hermaphroditism.

154
00:17:47,424 --> 00:17:51,362
In these cases, different gonad types can be paired.

155
00:17:51,362 --> 00:17:59,887
Pair combinations may include a normal testis, a normal ovary, a streak gonad, a dysgenetic testis, or an ovotestis.

156
00:17:59,887 --> 00:18:05,837
In the last, both ovarian and testicular elements are combined within the same gonad.

157
00:18:05,837 --> 00:18:13,849
With ovotesticular cases, the internal ductal system structure depends on the type of ipsilateral gonad and its function.

158
00:18:13,849 --> 00:18:23,812
Specifically, the amount of AMH and testosterone determines the degree to which the internal ductal system is retained or reabsorbed (p. 35).

159
00:18:23,812 --> 00:18:28,574
With inadequate AMH, müllerian duct derivatives persist.

160
00:18:28,574 --> 00:18:35,424
With inadequate testosterone, external genitalia are usually ambiguous and undermasculinized.

161
00:18:35,424 --> 00:18:43,712
Ovotesticular development may be found in all three of the DSD categories, and each of these sections describes examples .

162
00:18:43,712 --> 00:18:46,274
# Germ Cell Cancer.

163
00:18:46,274 --> 00:18:52,537
This cancer can develop in dysgenetic gonads of patients bearing all or part of the Y chromosome.

164
00:18:52,537 --> 00:19:00,874
On the Y chromosome, the gene for testis-specific protein Y (TSPY) is one putative cancer-predisposing gene.

165
00:19:00,874 --> 00:19:08,662
Other risks include disturbed gonadal development, delayed germ cell maturation, and presence of a gonadoblastoma .

166
00:19:08,662 --> 00:19:14,312
The last is a benign tumor that contains germ cells and immature granulosa cells .

167
00:19:14,312 --> 00:19:24,499
Germ cell cancer (GCC) risk varies among DSD types, but for some, gonadectomy may be recommended by a multispecialty DSD team .

168
00:19:24,499 --> 00:19:34,787
Early surgery benefits those with higher GCC risk and those in whom gonadal hormones may run counter to preferred pubertal development for the individual’s chosen gender

169
00:19:34,948 --> 00:19:36,673
identity.

170
00:19:36,623 --> 00:19:47,160
Later surgery may benefit those with low GCC risk and in whom gonadal hormones would advance desired pubertal development (van der Zwan, 2015).

171
00:19:47,160 --> 00:19:53,423
In addition to dysgenetic gonads, undescended testes also have higher GCC rates .

172
00:19:53,423 --> 00:20:04,885
Instead of gonadectomy, orchiopexy, which translocates and fixes a testis into the scrotum, may be protective (Radmayr, 2016; Walsh, 2007).

173
00:20:04,885 --> 00:20:07,948
# Sex Chromosome DSD.

174
00:20:07,948 --> 00:20:17,710
Cases in this first DSD category typically arise from an abnormal number of sex chromosomes, that is, sex chromosome aneuploidy (SCA).

175
00:20:17,710 --> 00:20:23,010
The population rate approximates 1 case per 2500 births .

176
00:20:23,010 --> 00:20:32,473
Discussed fully in Chapter 17 (p. 333), prenatal screening methods allow detection of aneuploidies, including SCAs .

177
00:20:32,473 --> 00:20:47,160
Sonographically, a thick nuchal translucency or the presence of cystic hygromas is associated with an increased SCA risk (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; Reiss, 2017).

178
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:59,835
Cell-free DNA present in the maternal blood is another common aneuploidy screening tool (Fig. 16-14, p. 328) (Huang, 2018; Vogel, 2019).

179
00:20:59,835 --> 00:21:09,373
Its selection solely for gender identification and without a medical indication is not recommended by the American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics .

180
00:21:09,373 --> 00:21:16,935
Still, with cell-free DNA use for other indications, antenatal detection of SCAs will likely rise.

181
00:21:16,935 --> 00:21:23,535
Interpretation and management of findings is discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 335).

182
00:21:23,535 --> 00:21:30,910
Advantageously, early recognition of sex chromosome DSD offers an opportunity for patient education.

183
00:21:30,910 --> 00:21:36,573
Decisions to end or continue pregnancies are best guided by clinical geneticists.

184
00:21:36,573 --> 00:21:42,773
For continued pregnancies, prenatal diagnosis allows early postnatal interventions .

185
00:21:42,773 --> 00:21:50,923
Turner Syndrome This develops from de novo loss or severe structural abnormality of one X chromosome in a phenotypic female.

186
00:21:50,923 --> 00:21:54,760
Most affected fetuses are spontaneously aborted.

187
00:21:54,760 --> 00:21:59,198
However, 1 in 2000 female live births are affected .

188
00:21:59,198 --> 00:22:06,198
In survivors with Turner syndrome, phenotype varies but nearly all affected patients have short stature.

189
00:22:06,198 --> 00:22:20,898
Associated problems can include hypertension; cardiac abnormalities, especially aortic coarctation, bicuspid aortic valve, and QT-interval prolongation; and renal, skeletal, and otolaryngological anomalies.

190
00:22:20,898 --> 00:22:25,010
A webbed posterior neck results from cystic hygromas.

191
00:22:25,010 --> 00:22:34,185
Metabolic concerns are diabetes mellitus (DM), autoimmune thyroiditis, celiac disease, and elevated liver enzymes .

192
00:22:34,185 --> 00:22:44,160
Two streak ovaries are typically found, and this syndrome is the most common form of gonadal dysgenesis that leads to primary ovarian insufficiency (POI).

193
00:22:44,160 --> 00:22:50,685
POI is the depletion or dysfunction of ovarian follicles that leads to failing sex hormone production.

194
00:22:50,685 --> 00:22:55,473
The uterus and vagina are normal and respond to prescribed hormones .

195
00:22:55,473 --> 00:23:08,423
Despite eventual POI, women with Turner syndrome may conceive with assisted reproductive technologies (ART) and rarely spontaneously (Hovatta, 1999; Oktay, 2016).

196
00:23:08,423 --> 00:23:16,798
In gravidas with Turner syndrome, cardiac morbidity stems from aortic dissection, aortic valve stenosis, and hypertension.

197
00:23:16,798 --> 00:23:24,410
For this reason, maternal–fetal medicine preconceptional counseling and cardiologist evaluation is essential.

198
00:23:24,410 --> 00:23:29,698
For aortic dissection, risk rises as the aortic diameter increases.

199
00:23:29,698 --> 00:23:40,248
The ascending aortic size index (ASI), which is the aortic diameter divided by body surface area, better reflects risk because it accounts for short stature.

200
00:23:40,248 --> 00:23:54,098
Conception should be avoided with an ASI >2.5 cm/m2 or with an ASI of 2.0 to 2.5 cm/m2 plus comorbid aortic dissection risk factors.

201
00:23:54,098 --> 00:24:06,473
These include bicuspid aortic valve, transverse aortic arch elongation, aortic coarctation, and hypertension (Gravholt, 2017; Silberbach, 2018).

202
00:24:06,473 --> 00:24:15,848
The American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2012) recommends against pregnancy with an ASI >2 cm.

203
00:24:15,848 --> 00:24:21,235
Even without identified risk, aortic dissection can develop in pregnancy .

204
00:24:21,235 --> 00:24:42,573
If conception is planned, preconceptional evaluation includes echocardiography, combined computed tomography plus cardiac magnetic resonance (CMR) imaging of the heart/aorta, 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring, exercise testing to reveal exercise-induced hypertension, and electrocardiogram .

205
00:24:42,573 --> 00:24:44,973
Cardiac disorders may

206
00:24:45,124 --> 00:24:49,024
require surveillance with serial echocardiograms.

207
00:24:48,974 --> 00:24:55,874
This syndrome carries higher fetal rates of miscarriage, preterm birth, and small-for-gestational age.

208
00:24:55,874 --> 00:25:06,586
Preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, and cholestasis of pregnancy are other maternal risks (Dotters-Katz, 2016a; Grewal, 2021).

209
00:25:06,586 --> 00:25:13,886
Thus, baseline serum tests ideally assess renal, liver, and thyroid function and screen for DM .

210
00:25:13,886 --> 00:25:29,124
These complications and cephalopelvic disproportion account for cesarean delivery rates of 40 to 80 percent in gravidas with Turner syndrome (Cadoret, 2018; Campens, 2021; Dotters-Katz, 2016a).

211
00:25:29,124 --> 00:25:40,899
Klinefelter Syndrome This syndrome has an estimated prevalence of 1 case per 650 newborn males fetuses (Bojesen, 2003; Radicioni, 2010).

212
00:25:40,899 --> 00:25:51,599
With chromosome complement of 47,XXY, these individuals tend to be tall, undervirilized males with gynecomastia and two small, firm testes.

213
00:25:51,599 --> 00:25:57,399
They have greatly reduced fertility from hypogonadism due to gradual testicular failure.

214
00:25:57,399 --> 00:26:10,599
These men are at increased risk for mediastinal germ cell tumors, osteoporosis, hypothyroidism, DM, breast cancer, cardiovascular abnormalities, and neurobehavioral disorders .

215
00:26:10,599 --> 00:26:15,836
Affected fetuses may show a thick NT during first-trimester sonography .

216
00:26:15,836 --> 00:26:24,086
Antepartum risks include preterm birth, cesarean delivery, small-for-gestational-age fetus, and neonatal death .

217
00:26:24,086 --> 00:26:36,249
47,XXX and 47,XYY Karyotypes These rare karyotypes are found in 1 in 10,000 newborn females and 2 in 10,000 newborn males, respectively .

218
00:26:36,249 --> 00:26:42,936
Sonographically, fetuses with either karyotype may show a thick NT and fetal-growth restriction .

219
00:26:42,936 --> 00:27:02,236
Phenotypic range is wide, but both may be taller, show subtle facial and skeletal dysmorphisms, and display neurobehavioral problems such as autism spectrum and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorders (Bardsley, 2013; Urbanus, 2020; Widby 2016).

220
00:27:02,236 --> 00:27:10,049
Hormone levels and fertility are unaffected in most, although POI and semen abnormalities may be seen, respectively.

221
00:27:10,049 --> 00:27:22,036
Sex Chromosomal Ovotesticular DSD In the sex chromosome DSD group, ovotesticular DSD may arise from a 46,XX/46,XY karyotype.

222
00:27:22,036 --> 00:27:26,624
Here, an ovary, testis, or ovotestis may be paired.

223
00:27:26,624 --> 00:27:35,136
For others, ovotesticular DSD arises from a chromosomal mosaic such as 45,X/46,XY.

224
00:27:35,136 --> 00:27:44,411
With this karyotype, mixed gonadal dysgenesis shows a streak gonad on one side and either a dysgenetic testis or normal testis on the other.

225
00:27:44,411 --> 00:27:48,411
For this reason, phenotypic gender varies widely.

226
00:27:48,411 --> 00:27:51,699
# 46,XY DSD.

227
00:27:51,699 --> 00:28:01,461
Insufficient androgen exposure of a fetus destined to be a male leads to 46,XY DSD—formerly called male pseudohermaphroditism.

228
00:28:01,461 --> 00:28:08,161
Testes are often present, and the uterus is generally absent due to the normal action of AMH.

229
00:28:08,161 --> 00:28:15,711
These individuals are usually sterile from abnormal spermatogenesis and a small phallus that is inadequate for coitus.

230
00:28:15,711 --> 00:28:25,711
Described next, the etiology of 46,XY DSD may stem from abnormal testis development or from abnormal androgen production or action .

231
00:28:25,711 --> 00:28:35,074
Rarely, 46,XY DSD may be part of another metabolic syndrome that alters structural development, such as Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome .

232
00:28:35,074 --> 00:28:46,111
46,XY Gonadal Dysgenesis This spectrum of abnormal gonad underdevelopment includes complete, partial, or mixed 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis.

233
00:28:46,111 --> 00:28:51,349
These are defined by karyotype and by the amount of abnormal testicular tissue.

234
00:28:51,349 --> 00:28:57,936
Because of the potential for GCC, gonadectomy is often recommended (p. 36).

235
00:28:57,936 --> 00:29:06,774
Of these, complete gonadal dysgenesis results from a mutation in the SRY gene or in other genes with testis-determining effects .

236
00:29:06,774 --> 00:29:14,836
Swyer syndrome reflects SRY defects, whereas Frasier and Denys-Drash syndromes have WT1 mutations.

237
00:29:14,836 --> 00:29:26,411
With both mutations, underdeveloped dysgenetic gonads fail to produce androgens or AMH, which results a normal prepubertal female phenotype and a normal müllerian system.

238
00:29:26,411 --> 00:29:33,886
Partial gonadal dysgenesis defines those with gonad development intermediate between normal and dysgenetic testes.

239
00:29:33,886 --> 00:29:41,411
Depending on percentages, wolffian and müllerian structures and genital ambiguity are variably expressed.

240
00:29:41,411 --> 00:29:46,286
Mixed gonadal dysgenesis is one type of ovotesticular DSD.

241
00:29:46,286 --> 00:29:53,799
As discussed in the last section, one gonad is streak, and the other is a normal testis or a dysgenetic testis.

242
00:29:53,799 --> 00:29:56,486
The phenotype is wide ranging.

243
00:29:56,644 --> 00:30:01,494
Last, testicular regression can follow initial testis development .

244
00:30:01,444 --> 00:30:06,519
The phenotypic spectrum is broad and depends on the timing of testis failure.

245
00:30:06,519 --> 00:30:23,456
Abnormal Androgen Production or Action Some cases of 46,XY DSD stem from abnormalities in: (1) testosterone biosynthesis, (2) LH-receptor function, (3) AMH function, or (4) androgenreceptor action.

246
00:30:23,456 --> 00:30:30,906
First, the sex-steroid biosynthesis pathway can suffer enzymatic defects that block testosterone production.

247
00:30:30,906 --> 00:30:37,731
Depending on blockade timing and degree, undervirilized males or phenotypic females may result.

248
00:30:37,731 --> 00:30:43,806
In contrast to these central enzymatic defects, peripheral defects can be causative.

249
00:30:43,806 --> 00:30:52,969
Namely, abnormal action of 5α-reductase type 2 enzyme impairs conversion of testosterone to DHT and blunts virilization.

250
00:30:52,969 --> 00:31:02,681
Second, hCG/LH receptor abnormalities within the testes can lead to Leydig cell hypoplasia and decreased testosterone production.

251
00:31:02,681 --> 00:31:10,881
In contrast, disorders of AMH and AMH receptors result in persistent müllerian duct syndrome (PMDS).

252
00:31:10,881 --> 00:31:17,831
These patients appear as males but have a persistent uterus and fallopian tubes due to failed AMH action.

253
00:31:17,831 --> 00:31:25,094
Last, the androgen receptor may be defective and result in androgeninsensitivity syndrome (AIS).

254
00:31:25,094 --> 00:31:32,331
Resistance to androgens may be incomplete and result in varying degrees of virilization and genital ambiguity.

255
00:31:32,331 --> 00:31:37,994
Milder forms may lead to poorly virilized men with severe male-factor infertility.

256
00:31:37,994 --> 00:31:44,494
Those with complete androgen-insensitivity syndrome (CAIS) are phenotypically normal females.

257
00:31:44,494 --> 00:31:48,681
Girls often present at puberty with primary amenorrhea.

258
00:31:48,681 --> 00:31:59,481
External genitalia appear normal; pubic and axillary hair are scant or absent; the vagina is markedly shortened; and the uterus and fallopian tubes are absent.

259
00:31:59,481 --> 00:32:06,194
However, these individuals develop breasts during puberty due to conversion of androgen to estrogen.

260
00:32:06,194 --> 00:32:11,506
Testes may be palpable in the labia or groin or may lie intraabdominally.

261
00:32:11,506 --> 00:32:14,506
Traditionally, testes are removed.

262
00:32:14,506 --> 00:32:24,206
However, women with AIS have a low risk of GCC, and retention until after puberty allows hormone-mediated breast and bone mass development.

263
00:32:24,206 --> 00:32:31,869
Despite hormone replacement after ultimate gonadectomy, many individuals describe hormone-related mood imbalance.

264
00:32:31,869 --> 00:32:40,206
New cancersurveillance protocols may allow gonad retention (Cools, 2017; Weidler, 2019).

265
00:32:40,206 --> 00:32:43,506
# 46,XX DSD.

266
00:32:43,506 --> 00:32:49,981
This DSD group may stem from abnormal ovarian development or from excess androgen exposure.

267
00:32:49,981 --> 00:33:04,419
Abnormal Ovarian Development Disorders of ovarian development in those with a 46,XX complement include: (1) gonadal dysgenesis, (2) testicular DSD, and (3) ovotesticular DSD.

268
00:33:04,419 --> 00:33:11,381
With 46,XX gonadal dysgenesis, similar to Turner syndrome, streak gonads develop.

269
00:33:11,381 --> 00:33:18,556
These lead to hypogonadism, prepubertal normal female genitalia, and normal müllerian structures.

270
00:33:18,556 --> 00:33:22,081
However, other Turner stigmata are absent.

271
00:33:22,081 --> 00:33:29,419
With 46,XX testicular DSD, several genetic mutations lead to testis-like formation.

272
00:33:29,419 --> 00:33:36,119
Most commonly, defects stem from SRY translocation onto one paternal X chromosome.

273
00:33:36,119 --> 00:33:41,144
Less often, other genes with testis-determining effects are activated.

274
00:33:41,144 --> 00:33:50,906
Regardless, AMH prompts müllerian system regression, and androgens promote wolffian system development and external genitalia masculinization.

275
00:33:50,906 --> 00:33:58,106
Spermatogenesis, however, is absent because needed genes on the long arm of the Y chromosome are lacking.

276
00:33:58,106 --> 00:34:04,194
These persons are not usually diagnosed until puberty or during infertility evaluation.

277
00:34:04,194 --> 00:34:15,431
With 46,XX ovotesticular DSD, individuals possess a unilateral ovotestis with a contralateral ovary or testis, or bilateral ovotestes.

278
00:34:15,431 --> 00:34:22,994
An overexpression of SOX genes, which are testis promoting, or deficient ovarian promoting genes are implicated .

279
00:34:22,994 --> 00:34:27,456
Phenotypic findings depend on the degree of androgen exposure.

280
00:34:27,456 --> 00:34:37,844
Androgen Excess Discordance between female gonadal sex and phenotypically masculine external genitalia may also result from excessive fetal androgen exposure.

281
00:34:37,844 --> 00:34:41,556
The prior term was female pseudohermaphroditism.

282
00:34:41,556 --> 00:34:50,256
In affected individuals, the ovaries and female internal ductal structures such as the uterus, cervix, and upper vagina develop.

283
00:34:50,256 --> 00:34:53,431
Thus, patients are potentially fertile.

284
00:34:53,431 --> 00:34:58,769
The external genitalia, however, are variably virilized depending on the

285
00:34:58,924 --> 00:35:02,061
amount and timing of androgen exposure.

286
00:35:02,011 --> 00:35:10,399
The embryonic clitoris, labioscrotal folds, and urogenital sinus are commonly affected by elevated androgen levels.

287
00:35:10,399 --> 00:35:18,074
Virilization may range from modest clitoromegaly to posterior labial fusion and a phallus with a penile urethra.

288
00:35:18,074 --> 00:35:27,774
Degrees of virilization can be described by the Prader score, which ranges from 0 for a normal-appearing female to 5 for a normal, virilized male.

289
00:35:27,774 --> 00:35:37,886
The external genitalia score is another and similarly ranges from 0 to 12, respectively (Ahmed, 2000; van der Straaten, 2020).

290
00:35:37,886 --> 00:35:43,636
Fetal, placental, or maternal sources can provide the excessive androgen levels.

291
00:35:43,636 --> 00:35:53,811
Maternally derived androgen excess may come from virilizing ovarian tumors such as luteoma and Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor or from virilizing adrenal tumors.

292
00:35:53,811 --> 00:36:09,249
Fortunately, these neoplasms infrequently cause fetal effects because of the placental syncytiotrophoblast’s tremendous ability to convert C19 steroids—androstenedione and testosterone—into estradiol via the enzyme aromatase .

293
00:36:09,249 --> 00:36:16,474
As another source, drugs such as testosterone, danazol, and other androgen derivatives may virilize.

294
00:36:16,474 --> 00:36:23,361
Of fetal sources, exposure can arise from fetal congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH).

295
00:36:23,361 --> 00:36:30,261
This stems from a fetal enzyme deficiency in the steroidogenic pathway and leads to androgen accumulation.

296
00:36:30,261 --> 00:36:34,411
Most cases have a 21-hydroxylase deficiency.

297
00:36:34,411 --> 00:36:53,049
Rarely, others involve deficient 3βhydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, 17α-hydroxylase, cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme, P450 oxidoreductase, or 11β-hydroxylase (El-Maouche, 2017; Narasimhan, 2019).

298
00:36:53,049 --> 00:37:06,061
CAH is a frequent cause of virilization, and its approximate incidence in the United States is 1 case in 18,000 live births (Chan, 2013; Pearce, 2016).

299
00:37:06,061 --> 00:37:13,611
With CAH, phenotypes depend on the enzyme defect’s location in the steroidogenic pathway and its severity .

300
00:37:13,611 --> 00:37:23,124
Classically, deficient enzymes block corticosteroid production, which feeds back to raise adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) levels.

301
00:37:23,124 --> 00:37:29,624
This prompts increased levels of precursors, which detour into pathways that generate androgens.

302
00:37:29,624 --> 00:37:37,411
With severe deficiency, affected newborns also have blocked aldosterone production that leads to life-threatening salt wasting.

303
00:37:37,411 --> 00:37:44,474
This is typified by hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and hypovolemia .

304
00:37:44,474 --> 00:37:48,424
Other mutations may prompt fetal virilization alone .

305
00:37:48,424 --> 00:37:56,211
The mildest abnormalities present later and are described as “nonclassic,” “late-onset,” or “adult-onset” CAH.

306
00:37:56,211 --> 00:38:04,936
In these patients, adrenal axis activation at puberty increases steroidogenesis and unmasks mild enzymatic deficiency.

307
00:38:04,936 --> 00:38:13,174
Excess androgen provides negative feedback to gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) receptors in the hypothalamus.

308
00:38:13,174 --> 00:38:18,061
These patients often have hirsutism, acne, and anovulation.

309
00:38:18,061 --> 00:38:23,099
Thus, late-onset CAH may mimic polycystic ovarian syndrome .

310
00:38:23,099 --> 00:38:27,836
In some instances, CAH can be diagnosed antenatally.

311
00:38:27,836 --> 00:38:34,049
Early maternal dexamethasone therapy can dampen androgen excess to minimize virilization .

312
00:38:34,049 --> 00:38:38,211
Cell-free DNA can identify fetal gonadal gender.

313
00:38:38,211 --> 00:38:47,011
If Ychromosome cell-free DNA is identified, androgens will not harm the male fetus and maternal dexamethasone treatment can be stopped .

314
00:38:47,011 --> 00:38:59,449
Of rare placental sources, placental aromatase deficiency from a fetal CYP19 gene mutation causes accumulation of placental androgen and underproduction of placental estrogens .

315
00:38:59,449 --> 00:39:04,924
Consequently, both the mother and the 46,XX fetus are virilized.

316
00:39:04,924 --> 00:39:07,461
# Gender Assignment.

317
00:39:07,461 --> 00:39:18,686
Delivery of a newborn with a DSD is a potential medical emergency and can also create possible long-lasting psychosexual and social ramifications for the individual and family.

318
00:39:18,686 --> 00:39:24,524
Ideally, once the affected neonate is stable, parents are encouraged to hold the child.

319
00:39:24,524 --> 00:39:34,986
The newborn is referred to as “your baby,” and suggested terms include “phallus,” “gonads,” “folds,” and “urogenital sinus” to reference underdeveloped structures.

320
00:39:34,986 --> 00:39:45,649
An obstetrician explains that the genitalia are incompletely formed and emphasizes the situation’s seriousness and need for rapid consultation and laboratory testing.

321
00:39:45,649 --> 00:39:52,136
Because similar or identical phenotypes may have several etiologies, identification of a

322
00:39:52,300 --> 00:39:56,875
specific DSD may require several diagnostic tools .

323
00:39:56,825 --> 00:40:10,687
Relevant historical questions seek prior obstetrical outcomes, medication inventory, consanguinity, germane antenatal testing and sonography results, and family history of genetic or structural anomalies.

324
00:40:10,687 --> 00:40:14,287
Signs of maternal hyperandrogenism are sought.

325
00:40:14,287 --> 00:40:39,262
Neonatal physical examination evaluates: (1) ability to palpate gonads in the labioscrotal or inguinal regions, (2) ability to palpate uterus during rectal examination, (3) phallus size, (4) other syndromic features, and (5) genitalia pigmentation, which derives from increased melanocyte-stimulating hormone levels that can accompany ACTH secretion.

326
00:40:39,262 --> 00:40:47,850
The newborn metabolic condition is assessed, and hyperkalemia, hyponatremia, and hypoglycemia may indicate CAH.

327
00:40:47,850 --> 00:40:57,862
Other neonatal tests include genetic studies, hormone measurements, imaging, and in some cases endoscopic, laparoscopic, and gonadal biopsy.

328
00:40:57,862 --> 00:41:09,850
Sonography or magnetic resonance (MR) imaging can help identify müllerian/wolffian structures, gonad location, and associated malformations such as renal anomalies.

329
00:41:09,850 --> 00:41:12,962
Bladder And Perineal Abnormalities.

330
00:41:12,962 --> 00:41:22,162
During embryo formation, a bilaminar cloacal membrane lies at the caudal end of the germinal disc and forms the infraumbilical abdominal wall.

331
00:41:22,162 --> 00:41:31,687
Normally, an ingrowth of mesoderm between the ectodermal and endodermal layers leads to formation of the lower abdominal musculature and pelvic bones.

332
00:41:31,687 --> 00:41:36,850
Without this reinforcement, the cloacal membrane may prematurely rupture.

333
00:41:36,850 --> 00:41:45,962
Depending on the infraumbilical defect’s extent, cloacal exstrophy, bladder exstrophy, or epispadias may result, and all are rare.

334
00:41:45,962 --> 00:42:02,487
Cloacal exstrophy, also known as the omphalocele, bladder exstrophy, imperforate anus, spina bifida (OEIS) complex, affects approximately 1 in 300,000 live births (Keppler-Noreuil, 2007; Woo, 2010).

335
00:42:02,487 --> 00:42:11,312
With bladder exstrophy, the bladder lies outside the abdomen, is open, drains directly into amnionic fluid, and thus does not fill.

336
00:42:11,312 --> 00:42:21,987
Sonography in the first trimester may show a thin-walled, lower-abdominal cystic structure and a thick NT (Mallmann, 2014; Tonni, 2011).

337
00:42:21,987 --> 00:42:33,050
During second-trimester scans, findings are a midline, infraumbilical, anterior-abdominal-wall defect, failure to see the bladder, and associated OEIS defects .

338
00:42:33,050 --> 00:42:36,187
Prenatal karyotyping is recommended.

339
00:42:36,187 --> 00:42:41,612
Delivery route is usually dictated by the associated spina bifida defect.

340
00:42:41,612 --> 00:42:50,925
Postnatal repair is complex, and individuals may struggle with urinary and fecal incontinence and the challenges of neonatal gender assignment .

341
00:42:50,925 --> 00:42:59,112
With bladder exstrophy, associated findings often include abnormal external genitalia and a widened symphysis pubis.

342
00:42:59,112 --> 00:43:08,650
At the same time, however, the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries are typically normal except for occasional müllerian duct fusion defects.

343
00:43:08,650 --> 00:43:28,575
Although often not identified in affected fetuses, sonographic indicators are inability to see the bladder, solid mass between the umbilical arteries, low umbilical insertion into the abdomen, divergent pubic rami, normal amnionic fluid volume, and in males, a small penis with anteriorly displaced scrotum .

344
00:43:28,575 --> 00:43:40,650
The differential diagnosis includes bladder exstrophy or agenesis, bilateral ectopic ureters, patent urachus, cloacal exstrophy, and simple nonvisualization of the bladder.

345
00:43:40,650 --> 00:43:43,975
MR imaging may be a helpful adjunct .

346
00:43:43,975 --> 00:43:48,612
Fetal karyotyping is considered if genitalia are ambiguous.

347
00:43:48,612 --> 00:43:57,025
For an affected fetus, the prenatal course is typically routine, and cesarean delivery is reserved for obstetrical indications.

348
00:43:57,025 --> 00:44:12,825
For a gravida with bladder exstrophy herself, pregnancy is associated with greater risk for antepartum pyelonephritis, urinary retention, ureteral obstruction, pelvic organ prolapse, miscarriage, preterm birth, and breech presentation.

349
00:44:12,825 --> 00:44:18,687
The American Urological Association has published management guidelines for pregnancy .

350
00:44:18,687 --> 00:44:32,250
Due to the extensive adhesions from prior repair and altered anatomy typically encountered, some recommend planned early cesarean delivery at a tertiary center (Deans, 2012; Greenwell, 2003).

351
00:44:32,250 --> 00:44:40,100
Dy and coworkers (2015) described using paramedian abdominal wall and vertical uterine incisions.

352
00:44:40,100 --> 00:44:42,050
Epispadias

353
00:44:42,220 --> 00:44:55,270
without bladder exstrophy is rare and develops in association with other anomalies such as a widened, patulous urethra; absent or bifid clitoris; nonfused labial folds; and flattened mons pubis.

354
00:44:55,220 --> 00:45:00,620
Vertebral abnormalities and pubic symphysis diathesis are also common.

355
00:45:00,620 --> 00:45:03,095
Clitoral anomalies are rare.

356
00:45:03,095 --> 00:45:11,532
One is clitoral duplication or bifid clitoris, which usually develops in association with bladder exstrophy or epispadias.

357
00:45:11,532 --> 00:45:16,445
With female phallic urethra, the urethra opens at the clitoral tip.

358
00:45:16,445 --> 00:45:23,795
Last, clitoromegaly noted at birth suggests fetal exposure to excessive androgens (p. 38).

359
00:45:23,795 --> 00:45:34,182
Without a DSD, idiopathic congenital clitoromegaly in females born extremely premature is rare but well-recognized, and observation is recommended .

360
00:45:34,182 --> 00:45:42,007
As noted, the hymen marks the embryological boundary between structures derived from the müllerian and urogenital sinus.

361
00:45:42,007 --> 00:45:50,882
Hymeneal anomalies include imperforate, microperforate, cribriform (sievelike), navicular (boat-shaped), and septate hymens.

362
00:45:50,882 --> 00:45:58,032
They result from failure of the inferior end of the vaginal plate—the hymeneal membrane—to canalize.

363
00:45:58,032 --> 00:46:07,907
Rarely, with an imperforate hymen, secretions may markedly accumulate in the fetal uterus and vagina, that is, hydrometrocolpos (HMC).

364
00:46:07,907 --> 00:46:15,957
Most cases of HMC are asymptomatic and resolve postnatally as mucus is reabsorbed and estrogen levels decline.

365
00:46:15,957 --> 00:46:23,820
Rarely, perinatal urinary tract obstruction results from mass effect and is relieved with cruciate incision of the hymen .

366
00:46:23,820 --> 00:46:29,120
Fetal HMC sonographically appears as a cystic mass behind the bladder.

367
00:46:29,120 --> 00:46:48,107
The differential diagnosis includes reproductive tract outlet obstruction of the hymen, vagina, or cervix; ureterocele; megacystis; ovarian, urachal, or mesenteric cyst; anterior meningocele; bowel or bladder duplications; and urogenital sinus or cloacal dysgenesis.

368
00:46:48,107 --> 00:46:54,595
Rare associated syndromes are McKusick-Kaufman, Ellis-van Creveld, or Bardet-Biedl syndromes .

369
00:46:54,595 --> 00:47:19,770
MÜLLERIAN ABNORMALITIES Four principal abnormalities arise from defective müllerian duct embryological steps: (1) agenesis of both ducts, either focally or along the entire duct length; (2) unilateral maturation of one müllerian duct with incomplete or absent development of the opposite side; (3) absent or faulty midline fusion of the ducts; or (4) defective canalization.

370
00:47:19,770 --> 00:47:28,832
Various classifications have been proposed, and Table 3-3 shows the one from the American Fertility Society (1988).

371
00:47:28,832 --> 00:47:40,270
This classification system is the most widely used, although several others have been created (Grimbizis, 2013, 2016; Ludwin, 2018b).

372
00:47:40,270 --> 00:47:42,482
Table 3-3.

373
00:47:42,482 --> 00:47:52,257
Classification of Müllerian Anomalies A müllerian anomaly is suspected if a vaginal septum, blind-ending vagina, or duplicated cervix is found.

374
00:47:52,257 --> 00:47:57,895
Amenorrhea may be an initial complaint for those with agenesis of a müllerian component.

375
00:47:57,895 --> 00:48:13,020
In those with outlet obstruction but functioning endometrium, pelvic pain may arise from occult blood that accumulates and distends the vagina, uterus, or fallopian tubes (Kapczuk, 2018; Patel, 2016).

376
00:48:13,020 --> 00:48:21,470
Endometriosis and its associated dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and chronic pain are also frequent with outlet obstruction .

377
00:48:21,470 --> 00:48:24,582
# Comorbid Renal Anomalies.

378
00:48:24,582 --> 00:48:34,245
Renal anomalies most frequently accompany unicornuate uterus, uterine didelphys, and anomalies with an ipsilateral obstructive vaginal septum.

379
00:48:34,245 --> 00:48:39,920
Less often, partial bicornuate, and partial septate uteri are associated .

380
00:48:39,920 --> 00:48:49,320
When müllerian anomalies are identified, the urinary system can be evaluated with sonography, MR imaging, or intravenous pyelography .

381
00:48:49,320 --> 00:48:54,520
The last two are advantageous because ureteral anatomy can be affected too.

382
00:48:54,520 --> 00:48:58,582
An absent unilateral kidney is the most common finding.

383
00:48:58,582 --> 00:49:08,207
On the other hand, if renal agenesis is found first, reproductive-tract imaging in early puberty may help identify müllerian anomalies early .

384
00:49:08,207 --> 00:49:11,820
# Müllerian Agenesis (Class I).

385
00:49:11,820 --> 00:49:19,507
Class I segmental defects are caused by müllerian hypoplasia or agenesis as shown in Figure 3-5 .

386
00:49:19,507 --> 00:49:29,957
These developmental defects can affect the vagina, cervix, uterus, or fallopian tubes and may be isolated or may coexist with other müllerian anomalies.

387
00:49:29,957 --> 00:49:33,920
Study Newer classification Figure 3-5.

388
00:49:33,920 --> 00:49:36,482
Classification of müllerian

389
00:49:36,652 --> 00:49:38,389
anomalies.

390
00:49:38,339 --> 00:49:41,589
DES = diethylstilbestrol.

391
00:49:41,589 --> 00:49:44,639
# Vaginal Abnormalities.

392
00:49:44,639 --> 00:49:52,364
Of these, vaginal agenesis is the most profound and may be isolated or associated with other müllerian anomalies.

393
00:49:52,364 --> 00:50:03,589
One example is the MayerRokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome, in which upper vaginal agenesis is typically associated with uterine hypoplasia or agenesis.

394
00:50:03,589 --> 00:50:16,202
Less often, this syndrome also displays additional abnormalities and is known by the acronym MURCS (müllerian duct aplasia, renal aplasia, and cervicothoracic somite dysplasia) .

395
00:50:16,202 --> 00:50:22,127
The obstetrical significance of vaginal anomalies depends greatly on the degree of obstruction.

396
00:50:22,127 --> 00:50:29,227
Complete vaginal agenesis, unless corrected surgically, precludes pregnancy by vaginal intercourse.

397
00:50:29,227 --> 00:50:36,677
With MRKH syndrome, a functional vagina can be created, but uterine agenesis proscribes childbearing.

398
00:50:36,677 --> 00:50:44,614
In these women, however, ova can be retrieved for in vitro fertilization (IVF) and carriage by a surrogate mother .

399
00:50:44,614 --> 00:50:53,739
Uterine transplantation is experimental but holds promise for these women (Johannesson, 2021; Jones, 2019).

400
00:50:53,739 --> 00:51:03,402
Of other vaginal anomalies, congenital septa may form longitudinally or transversely, and each can arise from a fusion or resorption defect.

401
00:51:03,402 --> 00:51:08,564
A longitudinal septum divides the vagina into right and left portions.

402
00:51:08,564 --> 00:51:12,527
It may be complete and extend the entire vaginal length.

403
00:51:12,527 --> 00:51:17,964
A partial septum usually forms high in the vagina but may develop at lower levels.

404
00:51:17,964 --> 00:51:22,502
These septa are often associated with other müllerian anomalies .

405
00:51:22,502 --> 00:51:29,077
During examination, the provider can usually guide a speculum up along one side of the septum.

406
00:51:29,077 --> 00:51:40,439
Similarly, in labor, a complete longitudinal vaginal septum usually does not cause dystocia because the fetus can descend through one vaginal side, which dilates sufficiently.

407
00:51:40,439 --> 00:51:48,452
An incomplete distal longitudinal septum, however, may interfere with descent, and antepartum resection is preferred .

408
00:51:48,452 --> 00:51:54,489
Rarely, a woman with an incomplete distal longitudinal septum instead presents in labor.

409
00:51:54,489 --> 00:52:00,902
During second-stage labor, this septum usually becomes attenuated by pressure from the fetal head.

410
00:52:00,902 --> 00:52:11,227
After ensuring adequate analgesia, the attachment of the septum to the posterior vaginal wall is isolated between two clamps, transected, and ligated.

411
00:52:11,227 --> 00:52:20,602
Following placenta delivery, the superior attachment is similarly isolated between clamps and transected, while carefully avoiding urethral injury.

412
00:52:20,602 --> 00:52:24,939
A transverse septum poses an obstruction of variable thickness.

413
00:52:24,939 --> 00:52:30,214
It may develop at any depth within the vagina, but most lie in the lower third .

414
00:52:30,214 --> 00:52:36,002
Septa may or may not be perforate, and thus obstruction or infertility is possible.

415
00:52:36,002 --> 00:52:45,864
In labor, perforate strictures may be mistaken for the upper limit of the vaginal vault, and the septal opening is misidentified as an undilated cervix .

416
00:52:45,864 --> 00:52:54,539
If encountered during labor, and after the cervix has dilated completely, the head impinges on the septum and causes it to bulge downward.

417
00:52:54,539 --> 00:53:01,564
If the septum does not spontaneously yield, slightly stretching its opening usually leads to further dilation.

418
00:53:01,564 --> 00:53:11,627
Occasionally cruciate incisions that avoid the urethra and rectum are required to permit delivery (Blanton, 2003; Levin, 1963).

419
00:53:11,627 --> 00:53:16,614
For a thick transverse septum, cesarean delivery may be necessary.

420
00:53:16,614 --> 00:53:19,677
# Cervical Abnormalities.

421
00:53:19,677 --> 00:53:28,564
Developmental abnormalities of the cervix include partial or complete agenesis, duplication, or a longitudinal dividing septum.

422
00:53:28,564 --> 00:53:32,477
Complete agenesis is incompatible with pregnancy.

423
00:53:32,477 --> 00:53:42,539
IVF with gestational surrogacy or with transmyometrial embryo transfer are options (Al-Jaroudi, 2011; Xu, 2009).

424
00:53:42,539 --> 00:53:52,677
Instead, surgical correction by uterovaginal anastomosis successfully relieves outlet obstruction, but subsequent pregnancy and live birth rates are low .

425
00:53:52,677 --> 00:53:58,152
Significant complications including deaths have accompanied such corrective surgery.

426
00:53:58,152 --> 00:54:11,764
Some experts recommend hysterectomy for complete cervical agenesis and reserve reconstruction attempts for carefully selected patients with cervical dysgenesis (Roberts, 2011; Rock, 2010).

427
00:54:11,764 --> 00:54:14,752
# Uterine Abnormalities.

428
00:54:14,752 --> 00:54:22,389
From a large variety, a few of the more common congenital uterine malformations are shown in Table 3-3.

429
00:54:22,389 --> 00:54:26,502
Assessing an accurate population prevalence is difficult

430
00:54:26,668 --> 00:54:30,405
because the best diagnostic techniques are invasive.

431
00:54:30,355 --> 00:54:45,155
The prevalence found with imaging ranges from 0.4 to 10 percent, and rates in women with recurrent miscarriage are significantly higher (Byrne, 2000; Dreisler, 2014; Saravelos, 2008).

432
00:54:45,155 --> 00:54:56,068
In a general population, the most frequent finding is arcuate uterus, followed in descending order by septate, bicornuate, didelphic, and unicornuate classes .

433
00:54:56,068 --> 00:55:09,818
As a group, these anomalies pose greater risk for miscarriage, malpresentation, preterm birth, and poor fetal growth (Chan, 2011a; Hua, 2011; Reichman, 2009).

434
00:55:09,818 --> 00:55:14,118
Vaginal delivery is the preferred delivery route when feasible.

435
00:55:14,118 --> 00:55:19,780
The cervix of the pregnancy-containing uterus or horn will typically dilate sufficiently.

436
00:55:19,780 --> 00:55:25,605
Similarly, any proximal longitudinal septum will stretch to permit fetal descent.

437
00:55:25,605 --> 00:55:36,018
Müllerian uterine anomalies may be discovered first during pelvic examination, cesarean delivery, tubal sterilization, or infertility evaluation.

438
00:55:36,018 --> 00:55:47,555
Depending on clinical presentation, sonography, hysterosalpingography (HSG), MR imaging, laparoscopy, and hysteroscopy may also be diagnostic.

439
00:55:47,555 --> 00:55:52,543
Each has limitations and thus may be combined to completely define anatomy.

440
00:55:52,543 --> 00:56:02,330
If a müllerian anomaly is suspected, two-dimensional transvaginal sonography (2-D TVS) is initially performed in most clinical settings.

441
00:56:02,330 --> 00:56:08,655
For this indication, the pooled accuracy for 2-D TVS is 90 to 92 percent .

442
00:56:08,655 --> 00:56:17,380
Three-dimensional (3-D) TVS is more accurate than 2-D TVS because it provides uterine images from virtually any angle.

443
00:56:17,380 --> 00:56:22,755
The diagnostic accuracy of 3-D TVS approaches 97 percent .

444
00:56:22,755 --> 00:56:31,093
Thus, coronal images can be constructed, and these are essential in evaluating both internal and external uterine contours.

445
00:56:31,093 --> 00:56:35,943
Both 2-D and 3-D TVS are suitable for use in pregnancy .

446
00:56:35,943 --> 00:56:38,318
Figure 3-6.

447
00:56:38,318 --> 00:56:44,418
A. Sagittal transvaginal sonogram shows a gestational sac surrounded by myometrium.

448
00:56:44,418 --> 00:56:51,068
The gestational sac is not contiguous with the endocervical canal or with the main horn’s endometrium.

449
00:56:51,068 --> 00:57:00,755
B. Here, two Pean clamps are placed across the vascular tissue bridge that separates the rudimentary horn (above) from the main uterine horn (below).

450
00:57:00,755 --> 00:57:04,955
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Elaine L. Duryea.

451
00:57:04,955 --> 00:57:16,330
Several studies reported good concordance between 3-D TVS and MR imaging of müllerian uterine anomalies (Deutch, 2008; Graupera, 2015).

452
00:57:16,330 --> 00:57:23,718
MR imaging is often preferred for complex anatomy, especially cases for which corrective surgery is planned.

453
00:57:23,718 --> 00:57:39,243
MR imaging provides clear delineation of both the internal and external uterine anatomy and has a reported accuracy of up to 100 percent for müllerian anomaly evaluation (Bermejo, 2010; Pellerito, 1992).

454
00:57:39,243 --> 00:57:46,318
Moreover, secondary diagnoses such as renal or skeletal anomalies can be concurrently evaluated.

455
00:57:46,318 --> 00:57:50,330
MR imaging without contrast is safe in pregnancy.

456
00:57:50,330 --> 00:57:58,943
Saline infusion sonography (SIS) is a technique that instills fluid into the uterine cavity to distend and define cavity shape.

457
00:57:58,943 --> 00:58:02,993
It improves delineation of internal uterine morphology.

458
00:58:02,993 --> 00:58:06,993
However, SIS is contraindicated in pregnancy.

459
00:58:06,993 --> 00:58:10,368
It requires a patent endometrial cavity.

460
00:58:10,368 --> 00:58:18,793
In women undergoing fertility evaluation, HSG is usually selected for uterine cavity and tubal patency assessment.

461
00:58:18,793 --> 00:58:21,943
It is contraindicated during pregnancy.

462
00:58:21,943 --> 00:58:28,468
HSG poorly defines the external uterine contour and can delineate only patent cavities.

463
00:58:28,468 --> 00:58:34,118
Described next, remember that some unicornuate rudimentary horns lack a cavity.

464
00:58:34,118 --> 00:58:38,005
Also, outlet obstructions will preclude dye filling.

465
00:58:38,005 --> 00:58:47,305
For infertility, hysteroscopy and/or laparoscopy plus chromotubation may help confirm or treat uterine cavity or tubal pathologies.

466
00:58:47,305 --> 00:58:54,705
These also screen for endometriosis, which often coexists with both infertility and müllerian anomalies .

467
00:58:54,705 --> 00:59:02,680
In pregnancy, laparoscopy is rarely used to diagnose müllerian defects, and hysteroscopy is contraindicated.

468
00:59:02,680 --> 00:59:10,768
Unicornuate Uterus (Class II) General population estimates cite an incidence of 1 case in 4000 women .

469
00:59:10,768 --> 00:59:16,943
In class II-D forms, only one uterine horn is present (see Fig. 3-5).

470
00:59:16,943 --> 00:59:21,393
Instead, an underdeveloped rudimentary horn may be present.

471
00:59:21,393 --> 00:59:28,643
The rudiment may or may not communicate with the dominant horn and may or may not contain an endometrium-lined cavity.

472
00:59:28,643 --> 00:59:30,068
With

473
00:59:30,220 --> 00:59:39,695
noncommunicating types, the rudiment may lie near the uterus or may lie anywhere along the embryological migration path of the paramesonephros.

474
00:59:39,645 --> 00:59:43,895
This starts at the back and sweeps forward along the broad ligament.

475
00:59:43,895 --> 00:59:55,795
This anomaly may be detected during fertility evaluation by HSG, but false-negative examinations stem from noncavitary or noncommunicating horns failing to fill with dye.

476
00:59:55,795 --> 01:00:05,282
If this anomaly is suspected, 3-D TVS raises diagnostic accuracy, but again MR imaging may be preferred to define endometrium .

477
01:00:05,282 --> 01:00:10,332
Importantly, 40 percent of affected women will have renal anomalies .

478
01:00:10,332 --> 01:00:12,107
Main Horn.

479
01:00:12,107 --> 01:00:17,307
Pregnancies developing in the main horn carry significant obstetrical risks.

480
01:00:17,307 --> 01:00:34,045
These include first- and second-trimester miscarriage, malpresentation, fetal-growth restriction, fetal demise, prematurely ruptured membranes, and preterm delivery (Chan, 2011a; Hua, 2011; Reichman, 2009).

481
01:00:34,045 --> 01:00:43,070
These risks theoretically stem from abnormal uterine blood flow, cervical insufficiency, and diminished cavity size and muscle mass .

482
01:00:43,070 --> 01:00:49,645
The main unicornuate horn is indeed smaller than normal uterine lengths of 7- to 8-cm .

483
01:00:49,645 --> 01:00:56,232
One small study of 140 nulligravidas found a median uterine length of 5 cm .

484
01:00:56,232 --> 01:01:07,870
Those with lengths from the internal os to fundus <4.5 cm prior to pregnancy had higher preterm labor rates during subsequent pregnancy compared with longer hemiuteri.

485
01:01:07,870 --> 01:01:12,357
Thus, a heightened awareness of potential complications is prudent.

486
01:01:12,357 --> 01:01:20,357
However, specific surveillance for poor fetal growth or preterm labor is mainly guided by prior pregnancy outcomes.

487
01:01:20,357 --> 01:01:22,420
Rudimentary Horn.

488
01:01:22,420 --> 01:01:28,395
Ectopic pregnancy, correctly termed a cornual pregnancy, can develop within a remnant .

489
01:01:28,395 --> 01:01:37,895
This risk includes noncommunicating cavitary rudiments, for which transperitoneal sperm migration permits ovum fertilization and pregnancy .

490
01:01:37,895 --> 01:01:45,082
Although less common than other unicornuate pregnancy complications, rupture can create life-threatening hemorrhage.

491
01:01:45,082 --> 01:01:54,520
Convergence of uterine and ovarian branches near the pregnancy and the commonly associated placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) explain this risk.

492
01:01:54,520 --> 01:02:05,920
In a report of 70 such pregnancies, Rolen and associates (1966) found that most rudimentary uterine horn pregnancies ruptured prior to 20 weeks’ gestation.

493
01:02:05,920 --> 01:02:15,920
Nahum (2002) reviewed the literature from 1900 to 1999 and identified 588 rudimentary horn pregnancies.

494
01:02:15,920 --> 01:02:21,245
Half had uterine rupture, and 80 percent did so before the third trimester.

495
01:02:21,245 --> 01:02:27,120
Of the total 588, the neonatal survival rate was only 6 percent.

496
01:02:27,120 --> 01:02:33,470
First-trimester sonography allows an earlier diagnosis and rudiment excision before rupture.

497
01:02:33,470 --> 01:02:42,882
The main horn shows an empty endometrium continuous with the cervical canal, and the interstitial portion of a fallopian tube is seen only on one side.

498
01:02:42,882 --> 01:03:08,220
The rudimentary horn pregnancy displays: (1) no continuity between the cervical canal and gestational sac, (2) myometrium surrounding the gestation, (3) PAS-associated hypervascularity surrounding the gestational sac, and (4) a vascular pedicle connecting the main horn and the sac’s surrounding myometrium (Mavrelos, 2007; Tsafrir, 2005).

499
01:03:08,220 --> 01:03:12,682
If necessary, 3-D TVS is an appropriate adjunct .

500
01:03:12,682 --> 01:03:15,645
As seen in Figure 3-6.

501
01:03:15,645 --> 01:03:19,157
the connecting pedicle can be broad and vascular.

502
01:03:19,157 --> 01:03:24,332
Treatment is surgical and removes the rudimentary horn and in situ pregnancy.

503
01:03:24,332 --> 01:03:33,995
The ipsilateral fallopian tube is also excised to avert future ectopic pregnancies (Dove, 2017; Worley, 2008).

504
01:03:33,995 --> 01:03:42,332
Steps include sequential division of the uteroovarian ligament, mesosalpinx, round ligament, and pedicle to the main horn.

505
01:03:42,332 --> 01:03:50,082
Ideally, the ovary is spared, but large pregnancies with a short uteroovarian ligament may prompt adnexectomy.

506
01:03:50,082 --> 01:03:55,632
Surgical route is dictated by pregnancy size and laparoscopic capabilities.

507
01:03:55,632 --> 01:04:00,832
In nonpregnant women, most unicornuate uteri are asymptomatic.

508
01:04:00,832 --> 01:04:07,545
Those with a noncommunicating cavitary rudiment may present with outlet obstruction symptoms at puberty.

509
01:04:07,545 --> 01:04:14,207
In this instance, comorbid endometriosis can club fimbria and obstruct tubal egress of blood.

510
01:04:14,207 --> 01:04:28,782
In all cases, prophylactic excision of a cavitary rudiment is recommended to avoid pregnancy in an inadequately sized horn (Fedele, 2005; Rackow, 2007; Schneiderman, 2018).

511
01:04:28,782 --> 01:04:30,195
Data

512
01:04:30,364 --> 01:04:34,526
regarding subsequent pregnancy after excision are scarce.

513
01:04:34,476 --> 01:04:39,351
In one series of eight women, all had a preterm cesarean delivery .

514
01:04:39,351 --> 01:04:54,664
Uterine Didelphys (Class III) This anomaly arises from incomplete fusion that results in two entirely separate hemiuteri, two cervices, and usually two vaginas or a longitudinal vaginal septum (see Fig. 3-5).

515
01:04:54,664 --> 01:05:06,239
Uterine didelphys may be isolated or part of a rare triad with an obstructed hemivagina and ipsilateral renal agenesis (OHVIRA), also known as Herlyn-Werner-Wunderlich syndrome .

516
01:05:06,239 --> 01:05:16,676
Rarely diagnosed antenatally, it is considered in a fetus with renal agenesis and a cystic pelvic mass, which reflects hydrometrocolpos (p. 40) .

517
01:05:16,676 --> 01:05:19,926
Fetal MR imaging aids diagnosis.

518
01:05:19,926 --> 01:05:28,176
Uterine didelphys is suspected on pelvic examination by identification of a longitudinal vaginal septum and two cervices.

519
01:05:28,176 --> 01:05:35,114
During HSG for fertility evaluation, contrast shows two separate endocervical canals.

520
01:05:35,114 --> 01:05:42,939
These open into separate noncommunicating fusiform endometrial cavities that each ends with a solitary fallopian tube.

521
01:05:42,939 --> 01:05:54,189
In women without fertility issues, 2- or 3-D TVS is a logical initial imaging tool, and separate divergent uterine horns with a large intervening fundal cleft are seen.

522
01:05:54,189 --> 01:05:58,876
Endometrial cavities and two cervices are uniformly separate.

523
01:05:58,876 --> 01:06:03,414
MR imaging can clarify cases lacking classic findings.

524
01:06:03,414 --> 01:06:11,901
Adverse obstetrical outcomes associated with uterine didelphys are similar to but less frequent than those seen with unicornuate uterus.

525
01:06:11,901 --> 01:06:21,076
Increased risks include miscarriage, preterm birth, and malpresentation (Chan, 2011a; Hua, 2011).

526
01:06:21,076 --> 01:06:30,126
Metroplasty for either uterine didelphys or bicornuate uterus involves resection of intervening myometrium and fundal recombination .

527
01:06:30,126 --> 01:06:39,276
These rarely performed surgeries are chosen for highly selected patients with otherwise unexplained recurrent miscarriage at later gestational ages.

528
01:06:39,276 --> 01:06:45,539
After metroplasty, scheduled delivery prior to labor is prudent to avoid uterine rupture .

529
01:06:45,539 --> 01:06:51,589
Bicornuate Uterus (Class IV) This fusion anomaly results in two hemiuteri.

530
01:06:51,589 --> 01:06:58,839
As shown in Figure 3-5, the central myometrium runs either partially or completely to the cervix.

531
01:06:58,839 --> 01:07:10,101
A complete bicornuate uterus may extend to the internal cervical os and have a single cervix (bicornuate unicollis) or reach the external os (bicornuate bicollis).

532
01:07:10,101 --> 01:07:15,914
As with uterine didelphys, a coexistent longitudinal vaginal septum is common.

533
01:07:15,914 --> 01:07:24,039
Radiological discrimination of a bicornuate uterus from a septate uterus can be challenging, and defining criteria vary.

534
01:07:24,039 --> 01:07:31,489
This distinction, however, is important because septate uterus can be treated with hysteroscopic septal resection.

535
01:07:31,489 --> 01:07:40,839
HSG or 2-D TVS may initially suggest an anomaly, but further distinction is provided by 3-D TVS or MR imaging.

536
01:07:40,839 --> 01:07:49,751
With these, the intercornual angle, fundal contour, and a straight line drawn between the imaged tubal ostia serve as defining thresholds .

537
01:07:49,751 --> 01:07:52,101
Figure 3-7.

538
01:07:52,101 --> 01:08:00,301
Three-dimensional transvaginal sonography (3-D TVS) images of müllerian uterine anomalies in the coronal plane.

539
01:08:00,301 --> 01:08:06,326
Below each are corresponding diagrammatic uteri and differing organizational definitions.

540
01:08:06,326 --> 01:08:08,826
A. Bicornuate uterus.

541
01:08:08,826 --> 01:08:18,564
This 3-D TVS rendered image demonstrates a concave external fundal contour that dips to a point near or below the interostial line (green line).

542
01:08:18,564 --> 01:08:22,564
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Elysia Moschos.

543
01:08:22,564 --> 01:08:25,001
B. Septate uterus.

544
01:08:25,001 --> 01:08:33,526
This sonogram depicts the narrow angle between the two small endometrial cavities, which is characteristic of a septate) uterus.

545
01:08:33,526 --> 01:08:38,426
The external contour here is flat but may show minor fundal indentation.

546
01:08:38,426 --> 01:08:40,976
C. Arcuate uterus.

547
01:08:40,976 --> 01:08:50,039
This image illustrates the normal external uterine contour and obtuse angle of the endometrial cleft that is characteristic of an arcuate uterus.

548
01:08:50,039 --> 01:08:56,826
Organizations vary among their use of the terms intercornual, interostial, or interstitial line.

549
01:08:56,826 --> 01:09:10,889
ASRM = American Society for Reproductive Medicine; ESHRE/ESGE = European Society of Human Reproduction and Embryology/European Society for Gynaecological Endoscopy.

550
01:09:10,889 --> 01:09:21,764
Data from American Society for Reproductive Medicine, 2016, Bermejo, 2010, Grimbizis, 2013, 2016.

551
01:09:21,764 --> 01:09:27,801
Bicornuate uterus carries increased risks for miscarriage, preterm birth, and

552
01:09:27,964 --> 01:09:34,114
malpresentation (Chan, 2011a; Mastrolia, 2017).

553
01:09:34,064 --> 01:09:41,664
As discussed in the prior section, rare surgical correction by metroplasty is reserved for highly selected cases.

554
01:09:41,664 --> 01:09:52,676
Septate Uterus (Class V) With this anomaly, a resorption defect leads to a persistent complete or partial longitudinal uterine septum (see Fig. 3-5).

555
01:09:52,676 --> 01:10:02,626
In the rare Robert uterus, an asymmetric longitudinal septum creates a sequestered noncommunicating hemicavity that acts similar to a rudimentary horn .

556
01:10:02,626 --> 01:10:09,401
Many septate uteri are identified during evaluation of infertility or recurrent pregnancy loss.

557
01:10:09,401 --> 01:10:21,514
Although an abnormality may be identified with HSG or 2-D TVS, typically 3-D TVS or MR imaging is required to differentiate septate and bicornuate uteri.

558
01:10:21,514 --> 01:10:26,751
Experts still debate diagnostic criteria (see Fig. 3-7).

559
01:10:26,751 --> 01:10:40,714
Septate anomalies can be associated with increased risks for adverse pregnancy outcomes that include miscarriage, preterm delivery, and malpresentation (Chan, 2011a; Ghi, 2012).

560
01:10:40,714 --> 01:10:55,526
In those with recurrent pregnancy loss, hysteroscopic septal resection may improve birth rates (American Society for Reproductive Medicine, 2016; Corroenne, 2018; Valle, 2013).

561
01:10:55,526 --> 01:11:08,851
During a subsequent labor, those with prior septal resection, especially if complicated by uterine perforation, carry a small risk for uterine rupture (Homer, 2000; Sentilhes, 2005).

562
01:11:08,851 --> 01:11:17,751
Arcuate Uterus (Class VI) This malformation is a mild deviation from the normally developed uterus (see Fig. 3-7).

563
01:11:17,751 --> 01:11:34,014
Most consider this anomaly benign, but some have found excessive second-trimester losses, preterm labor, and malpresentation (Chan, 2011a; Mucowski, 2010; Prior, 2018; Woelfer, 2001).

564
01:11:34,014 --> 01:11:41,951
Cesarean Delivery As noted in the above sections, cesarean delivery rates are increased with müllerian uterine anomalies.

565
01:11:41,951 --> 01:11:54,914
Data regarding subsequent trial of labor after cesarean (TOLAC) are few and show evidence both for and against its success and safety (Erez, 2007; Ravasia, 1999).

566
01:11:54,914 --> 01:12:08,926
Failed TOLAC attempts or the risk of rupture may stem from smaller than normal cavity size, abnormal propagation of myometrial action potentials, and a weaker cesarean scar due to congenitally altered vascular anatomy .

567
01:12:08,926 --> 01:12:17,214
Other factors that support or disfavor TOLAC are similar to those for women with normalshaped uteri and are outlined in Chapter 31.

568
01:12:17,214 --> 01:12:27,551
In those without prior cesarean delivery, external cephalic version (ECV) is reasonable to avoid primary cesarean delivery for malpresentation.

569
01:12:27,551 --> 01:12:33,164
A smaller uterine cavity or an obstructing midline partition may limit fetal turning.

570
01:12:33,164 --> 01:12:40,076
These are added to the traditional list of factors that should be reviewed when assessing a potential ECV candidate .

571
01:12:40,076 --> 01:12:48,739
At times, a müllerian anomaly may not be diagnosed until cesarean delivery, and additional information should be methodically sought.

572
01:12:48,739 --> 01:12:55,914
Prior to hysterotomy closure, the cavity is manually explored to define the length of any cavity partition.

573
01:12:55,914 --> 01:13:02,451
If a rudimentary horn is found, the main cavity is also digitally explored for a communication.

574
01:13:02,451 --> 01:13:06,451
However, this may be missed due to its narrow caliber.

575
01:13:06,451 --> 01:13:09,714
After closure, the fundus is examined.

576
01:13:09,714 --> 01:13:15,451
The external contour is delineated to differentiate bicornuate from didelphys types.

577
01:13:15,451 --> 01:13:20,526
If only one adnexum is found, a unicornuate uterus is suspected.

578
01:13:20,526 --> 01:13:32,826
In this case, if an obvious rudiment is not attached to the main horn, the surgeon should trace the embryological migratory path of the paramesonephric duct starting at the patient’s back and sweeping forward.

579
01:13:32,826 --> 01:13:41,439
If found, the rudiment may be removed or at minimum its accompanying fallopian tube should be ligated to prevent later ectopic pregnancy.

580
01:13:41,439 --> 01:13:48,164
With any müllerian anomaly, both renal fossae are examined intraoperatively to confirm kidneys.

581
01:13:48,164 --> 01:13:53,776
Postoperative radiologic assessment of the urinary collecting anatomy is reasonable.

582
01:13:53,776 --> 01:14:05,601
Treatment with Cerclage Uterine anomalies are one risk for cervical insufficiency (Althuisius, 2001; Berghella, 1999; Mastrolia, 2018).

583
01:14:05,601 --> 01:14:17,214
Some women with uterine anomalies and repetitive pregnancy loss after the first trimester may benefit from cervical cerclage (Golan, 1992; Yassaee, 2011).

584
01:14:17,214 --> 01:14:24,764
Others with partial cervical atresia or hypoplasia also may benefit (Ludmir, 1991; Song,

585
01:14:24,916 --> 01:14:46,391
2015). For women with a uterine anomaly, candidacy for cervical length sonographic screening or for cerclage placement is determined by the same criteria used for women without a uterine defect (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021; Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine, 2016).

586
01:14:46,391 --> 01:14:54,803
These topics are discussed in Chapters 45 (p. 794) and 11 (p. 205), respectively.

587
01:14:54,803 --> 01:14:59,791
# Diethylstilbestrol-related Abnormalities (Class VII).

588
01:14:59,791 --> 01:15:11,866
In the 1960s, a synthetic nonsteroidal estrogen—diethylstilbestrol (DES)— was used to treat threatened abortion, preterm labor, preeclampsia, and diabetes.

589
01:15:11,866 --> 01:15:14,516
It was remarkably ineffective.

590
01:15:14,516 --> 01:15:31,478
Moreover, women exposed as fetuses carry increased risks for vaginal clear cell adenocarcinoma, cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, and vaginal adenosis (Hatch, 2001; Herbst, 1971; Robboy, 1984).

591
01:15:31,478 --> 01:15:40,266
Women exposed in utero can also show a cervix or vagina with a transverse septum, circumferential ridge, or cervical collar.

592
01:15:40,266 --> 01:15:46,253
Uteri are potentially smaller or have a T-shaped cavity (see Fig. 3-5) .

593
01:15:46,253 --> 01:15:57,766
Women exposed as fetuses are now postreproductive, but they did suffer risks for infertility and adverse pregnancy outcome (Kaufman, 2000; Palmer, 2001).

594
01:15:57,766 --> 01:16:01,141
# Fallopian Tube Abnormalities.

595
01:16:01,141 --> 01:16:06,378
The fallopian tubes develop from the unpaired distal ends of the müllerian ducts.

596
01:16:06,378 --> 01:16:15,141
Congenital anomalies include accessory ostia, complete or segmental tubal agenesis, and several embryonic cystic remnants.

597
01:16:15,141 --> 01:16:23,191
The most common is a small, benign cyst attached by a pedicle to the distal end of the fallopian tube—the hydatid of Morgagni.

598
01:16:23,191 --> 01:16:29,641
In other cases, benign paratubal cysts may be of mesonephric or mesothelial origin.

599
01:16:29,641 --> 01:16:35,928
Last, in utero exposure to DES is associated with various tubal abnormalities .

600
01:16:35,928 --> 01:16:37,916
Uterine Flexion.

601
01:16:37,916 --> 01:16:46,078
Moderate flexion of the pregnant uterus is typically inconsequential, but exaggerated flexion may pose unique complications.

602
01:16:46,078 --> 01:16:52,903
Anteflexion describes forward angling of the uterine fundus in the sagittal plane relative to the cervix.

603
01:16:52,903 --> 01:17:00,066
In extreme cases, the fundus later in pregnancy falls forward to lie below the lower margin of the symphysis.

604
01:17:00,066 --> 01:17:03,416
Abdominal wall laxity is contributory.

605
01:17:03,416 --> 01:17:12,878
This uterine position can prevent proper transmission of labor contractions but is usually corrected by repositioning and application of an abdominal binder.

606
01:17:12,878 --> 01:17:18,366
Retroflexion describes uterine fundal angling posteriorly in the sagittal plane.

607
01:17:18,366 --> 01:17:24,191
A growing retroflexed uterus fundus will occasionally become trapped in the hollow of the sacrum .

608
01:17:24,191 --> 01:17:29,578
Symptoms include pelvic pressure or pain plus voiding dysfunction or retention.

609
01:17:29,578 --> 01:17:39,178
During bimanual pelvic examination, the cervix will be anterior and behind the symphysis pubis, whereas the uterus is wedged in the deep pelvis.

610
01:17:39,178 --> 01:17:47,253
Sonography or MR imaging can aid diagnosis (Gardner, 2013; Grossenburg, 2011).

611
01:17:47,253 --> 01:17:49,378
Figure 3-8.

612
01:17:49,378 --> 01:17:56,578
A. With uterine incarceration, the uterus is wedged between the sacral promontory and symphysis pubis.

613
01:17:56,578 --> 01:18:07,091
Resulting pressure against the urethra and rectum can cause urinary retention and constipation, respectively (Reproduced with permission from Corton MM: Anatomy.

614
01:18:07,091 --> 01:18:16,403
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

615
01:18:16,403 --> 01:18:20,891
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

616
01:18:20,891 --> 01:18:27,828
B. Sagittal transvaginal sonogram shows a first-trimester fetus and incarcerated uterine fundus.

617
01:18:27,828 --> 01:18:32,416
The marked retroflexion brings the fundus to lie beneath the cervix.

618
01:18:32,416 --> 01:18:36,353
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Angela Seasley.

619
01:18:36,353 --> 01:18:43,403
With continued uterine growth, the incarcerated uterus can spontaneously resolve over 1 to 2 weeks.

620
01:18:43,403 --> 01:18:49,116
A knee-chest position assumed by the patient several times daily may assist resolution .

621
01:18:49,116 --> 01:18:54,991
An indwelling urinary catheter or intermittent self-catheterization resolves retention.

622
01:18:54,991 --> 01:18:58,741
Persistent cases require manual repositioning.

623
01:18:58,741 --> 01:19:07,266
For this, after bladder catheterization, the uterus can usually be pushed out of the pelvis when the woman is placed in a knee-chest position.

624
01:19:07,266 --> 01:19:12,941
Often, this is best accomplished by digital pressure applied through the rectum or vagina.

625
01:19:12,941 --> 01:19:19,753
Intravenous sedation or spinal analgesia aids comfort and allows sufficient dislodging forces .

626
01:19:19,753 --> 01:19:23,566
Afterward, insertion of a soft, space-filling

627
01:19:23,716 --> 01:19:27,578
pessary for a few weeks usually prevents recurrence .

628
01:19:27,528 --> 01:19:38,728
For rare resistant cases, advancing a colonoscope or colonoscopic insufflation can dislodge the fundus (Newell, 2014; Seubert, 1999).

629
01:19:38,728 --> 01:19:43,953
Upward round ligament traction during laparoscopy also has been described .

630
01:19:43,953 --> 01:19:51,703
Rarely, sacculation may form as an extensive lower uterine segment dilation due to persistent uterine entrapment.

631
01:19:51,703 --> 01:19:57,741
Clinically, the elongated vagina extends above the level of the deeply descended fetal head.

632
01:19:57,741 --> 01:20:02,778
The Foley catheter is frequently palpated above the level of the umbilicus!

633
01:20:02,778 --> 01:20:11,553
In these extreme cases, sonography and MR imaging help define anatomy (Gottschalk, 2008; Lee, 2008).

634
01:20:11,553 --> 01:20:17,628
To avoid uterine rupture, cesarean delivery is necessary when sacculation is marked.

635
01:20:17,628 --> 01:20:27,941
Spearing (1978) recommended extending the abdominal incision above the umbilicus and delivering the entire uterus from the abdomen before hysterotomy.

636
01:20:27,941 --> 01:20:36,066
Correct anatomical relationships are ideally restored to help prevent inadvertent incisions into and through the vagina and bladder .

637
01:20:36,066 --> 01:20:40,316
Singh, 2007; Uma, 2002).

638
01:20:40,316 --> 01:20:42,628
Figure 3-9.

639
01:20:42,628 --> 01:20:51,528
With anterior sacculation, note the markedly attenuated anterior uterine wall and atypical location of the true uterine fundus.

640
01:20:51,528 --> 01:20:55,241
Uterine torsion is another rare acquired anomaly.

641
01:20:55,241 --> 01:21:00,616
During pregnancy, the uterus commonly rotates gently to the maternal right.

642
01:21:00,616 --> 01:21:15,528
Rotation exceeding 180 degrees creates torsion, and most cases stem from uterine leiomyomas, müllerian anomalies, fetal malpresentation, pelvic adhesions, or laxity of the abdominal wall or uterine ligaments.

643
01:21:15,528 --> 01:21:28,303
In one review of 212 cases, associated symptoms were obstructed labor, intestinal or urinary complaints, abdominal pain, uterine hypertonus, vaginal bleeding, or hypotension .

644
01:21:28,303 --> 01:21:38,178
In some women, torsion can be confirmed preoperatively with MR imaging, which shows a twisted vagina that appears X-shaped rather than its normal H-shape .

645
01:21:38,178 --> 01:21:44,691
Flipped placenta location and abnormal umbilical Doppler findings have been described sonographically .

646
01:21:44,691 --> 01:21:55,128
However, uterine torsion is usually found at the time of cesarean delivery, and the severely rotated uterus should be repositioned anatomically before hysterotomy.

647
01:21:55,128 --> 01:22:08,003
In some cases, an inability to reposition or a failure to recognize the torsion may lead to a posterior hysterotomy incision (Albayrak, 2011; Karavani, 2017).

648
01:22:08,003 --> 01:22:12,416
Rotation of 90 degrees risks uterine vessel laceration .

